why 1 3/4 DIRECT The tune leaps down G♯–E while the bass steps F–E within the same E chord. A hidden octave inside one harmony, with a step in the bass, is mild.
why 3 1/2 LEAPS After falling a sixth the tenor turns back up by a fifth to E, the root of the dominant at the fermata, rather than by a step.
why 8 3/4 DOUBLING A passing chord over the bass's B; its doubling follows the upper voices, which hold still while the bass climbs.
why 8 3/4 SIX_FOUR The bass climbs A–B–C♯–D by step into D minor, and the chord over B is a passing ⁶₄ on that line.
